WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:04.120
At first glance, the war in Ukraine...

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.560
...might look like something from the 20th century

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.280
You see a sort of trench-like landscape...

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:13.360
...that would be familiar to soldiers from 1915

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:17.160
But it holds many lessons for conflicts to come

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:20.200
While boots on the ground still matter...

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:24.880
Russia has moved to tighten its conscription law, pressing more men to fight

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.400
...new tech is making a huge difference

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:32.320
There’s this idea of revolutions in military affairs...

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.280
...and they’re typically attributed to technology

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:40.800
Drones will definitely be very important...

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.400
...in any conflict between Taiwan and China

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:48.280
As the world enters a new era of increased conflicts...

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.480
...what does the future of combat look like?

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:55.840
This may be the moment where war really does become something else

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:35.000
In Ukraine’s capital of Kyiv, Vitalii runs AirLogix...

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.000
...a company that makes drones for the Ukrainian military

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.040
Today he’s testing their flagship product...

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.320
...a reconnaissance drone called the “Gor”...

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.840
...as well as a smaller loitering munition, or kamikaze drone

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.160
Over 20 such systems have already been sent to the front line

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.160
It’s the successful culmination of months of research and development

00:02:18.040 --> 00:02:21.560
Whether drones are adapted to be used as weapons or as guides...

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:26.520
...both Russia and Ukraine are sending huge numbers onto the battlefield

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:30.360
We’re now estimating that the Ukrainian armed forces...

00:02:30.360 --> 00:02:33.160
...are losing some 10,000 drones a month

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:38.040
So just the extent to which drones are being used is definitely noteworthy

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.520
Drones have flown in wars before...

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:44.720
...but what’s different in Ukraine is how they’re being used

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.840
What I think it’s teaching us is that just because drones are being shot down...

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.720
...doesn’t mean they aren’t absolutely indispensable

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:55.160
If the drone is shot down, no problem. It’s done its job

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.200
These are almost becoming like consumables on the battlefield

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:38.360
Search-and-destroy drone missions are a huge part of the new tech...

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:39.920
...dominating the battlefield...

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.320
...but it’s artillery batteries, flying drones to show them where to aim their guns...

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.640
...that are using them with the most effect

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:55.920
Without expendable drones as their eyes and ears, shooters would be nearly blind

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.720
Just how many drones are being used is an invaluable lesson for any future conflict

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.640
Quantity can really matter in a war, and quantity has a quality of its own

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:08.560
That’s something that in the West we may have forgotten...

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:12.040
...to some extent, we have relatively shallow arsenals...

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:14.960
...with regard to ammunition and things like that

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:20.320
And now we’re realising that, yeah, in these kind of rather large conventional wars...

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:24.800
...you also just need a lot of drones and a lot of everything, really

00:04:24.800 --> 00:04:27.880
Whether they’re consumable or large and long-range...

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.480
...Ukraine has demonstrated drones will be regular players in wars of the future

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.560
But the intelligence drones collect is only useful...

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:39.280
...if there’s a means of communicating it to the wider force

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.000
And in the battlefield that isn’t always easy

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:49.400
Soldiers like Favorsky are at the forefront of that challenge

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:54.080
They risk their lives training soldiers how to use technology...

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:56.520
...that has become a lifeline for Ukraine

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:57.800
Starlink

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:11.200
Away from the battlefield, maintaining the equipment is essential

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:33.760
Starlink is a satellite constellation owned by SpaceX, an American rocket company

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:39.080
It’s made up of more than 4,000 low-orbit satellites...

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:42.080
...that provide off-grid internet access...

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:47.680
...a lifeline when heavy artillery is constantly battering your ground-based utilities

00:05:47.680 --> 00:05:49.840
And crucially, it’s allowing soldiers...

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:53.680
...on the ground to share images from drone feeds to connect targets...

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:55.920
...with the nearest artillery team...

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.520
...a kind of Uber for howitzers

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:16.040
Other countries are taking note

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:20.280
Chinese generals have been watching American wars since 1991...

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.040
...when America’s lightning-fast victory over Iraq...

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:27.880
...woke them up to the power of American intelligence...

00:06:27.880 --> 00:06:29.080
...and digital networks

00:06:29.080 --> 00:06:32.360
And from that moment on, the People’s Liberation Army of China realised...

00:06:32.360 --> 00:06:36.200
...not only that they had to be able to wage a similar kind of warfare...

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:37.840
...but that if they were going to be able...

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:42.000
...to defeat America and its allies in Asia, they would have to take out...

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:44.720
...not just weapons and soldiers on the ground...

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.240
...but you take out the systems that they rely on

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:52.680
Taiwan, constantly in China’s crosshairs, knows its undersea...

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:56.200
...internet cables could be cut in the event of an invasion

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:02.120
It too sees the value of a Starlink-type constellation in a future conflict

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:07.240
Satellite communications in the future are going to be...

00:07:07.240 --> 00:07:09.680
...that absolutely critical element...

00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:14.680
...that allows sensors to be connected to shooters in a timely fashion...

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:19.000
...in a way that ensures if a soldier sees a tank and sends that information back...

00:07:19.000 --> 00:07:22.440
...you can hit it, in a timescale that is minutes...

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:25.160
...not hours, by which the tank may be miles away

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:30.160
Starlink may help information move quicker and more securely...

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:32.680
...but in wars of the future...

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:36.280
...artificial intelligence will help to make sense of that information

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:40.720
Hello, I’m Tom Standage, deputy editor at The Economist

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:42.200
If you’re enjoying this film...

00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:44.880
...why not take out a subscription to The Economist?

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:49.120
You’ll receive daily and weekly analysis of global affairs...

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.760
...and you can read us online, in the app and listen to our audio edition

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:55.920
For the best offer click on the link

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:01.680
Generals in Ukraine are already adapting AI technology...

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:03.800
...to help them plan what to attack...

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:06.320
...what resources to use and when...

00:08:06.320 --> 00:08:08.560
...a kind of Google Maps for armies

00:08:09.360 --> 00:08:11.160
It’s another glimpse of how new tech...

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.480
...will be helping tomorrow’s soldiers

00:08:13.480 --> 00:08:16.680
You’ll use these virtual simulations for various...

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:19.360
...training or experimental purposes

00:08:20.600 --> 00:08:22.560
Jennifer McArdle should know

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:25.600
She specialises in training soldiers for future wars

00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:32.200
Ukraine has been a great example of how the ubiquity and proliferation of sensors...

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:36.240
...has allowed them to more effectively close kill chains

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.560
Shortening a kill chain means speeding up the time...

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:46.600
...between finding a target and destroying it

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:48.400
That’s a maritime patrol aircraft...

00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:50.320
...can you show me their position?

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:55.080
Satellites, radar and drones provide an overwhelming amount of data...

00:08:55.080 --> 00:09:00.280
...from the battlefield, and sorting through it is hugely time-intensive

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.840
If you’re able to use AI to go through that information...

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.080
...and to be able to present information...

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:11.200
...that matters for a decision-maker, you have speed

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:14.320
You can work through that decision-making cycle...

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:16.960
...much more quickly than you can right now

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:22.000
And AI isn’t just helping to make decisions

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:24.480
It’s also guiding the machines

00:09:26.520 --> 00:09:31.320
Shield AI is a military tech company working to build an “AI pilot”...

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:37.160
...artificial intelligence software that can fly unmanned craft during combat

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:40.440
The future of warfare is going to be characterised...

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:45.360
...by thousands of unmanned aerial vehicles, unmanned surface vehicles...

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:47.280
...unmanned underwater vehicles

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:50.640
And what is going to be the differentiating factor...

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:51.960
...among all these vehicles...

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:53.800
...is the level of intelligence...

00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:57.960
...the level of autonomy integrated into each one of those vehicles

00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:01.280
You’ll see unmanned systems outnumber...

00:10:01.280 --> 00:10:05.240
...the number of warfighters on the front lines of these conflicts

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:09.200
They will play a key role in any battle of the future

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:14.560
Business is booming for military startups

00:10:16.000 --> 00:10:17.640
Since 2021...

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:22.640
...the American defence department’s spending on AI allocated by financial year...

00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:24.120
...has more than doubled

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:28.200
And NATO is also heavily investing

00:10:28.960 --> 00:10:31.880
There has been a wave of venture capital...

00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:34.880
...that has gone to defence technology companies

00:10:34.880 --> 00:10:36.880
I think it’s because you see an industry...

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.120
...that is fundamentally transforming

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:40.680
The products and capabilities...

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:43.400
...that are going to matter in the next conflict...

00:10:43.400 --> 00:10:47.560
...are going to be derived from software capabilities from AI

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.400
But there are concerns about leaving the decision to kill to an algorithm

00:10:54.440 --> 00:10:58.920
There’s this worry, I think, in popular culture that AI...

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.400
...is going to spawn these terminators

00:11:01.400 --> 00:11:04.480
And while I think there is reason to be cautious...

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.520
...I do think it’s important we don’t engage in hyperbole

00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:12.040
In the US and in a lot of Western countries...

00:11:12.040 --> 00:11:14.760
...we have made the deliberate decision...

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.640
...that when it comes to decisions around...

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.920
...lethality, a human will always be in, or on, that decision-making loop

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:26.960
Where this, I think, does become a little bit worrisome...

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:30.200
...is that other countries haven’t made that same normative decision

00:11:31.640 --> 00:11:35.880
Over 100 countries have military systems with some degree of autonomy

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.240
It’s easy to see how a weapon that thinks for itself could be an advantage

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.800
If you are a commander and you launch...

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:51.080
...the weapon, let’s say at an aircraft, and its communications are jammed...

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.920
...if it spots a target on the ground...

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:56.000
...are you going to wait for your communication link to be restored...

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.160
...by which time the target may have gone away?

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.760
Or are you going to give the weapon...

00:11:59.760 --> 00:12:01.880
...the authority to take the shot itself?

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.320
From a purely military perspective...

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:09.600
...that independence is hugely valuable under those kinds of...

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:12.600
...comms-denied environments

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:17.920
But for now, humans largely maintain lethal control

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:25.560
I’m sceptical that AI on its own can turn the tide of war

00:12:25.560 --> 00:12:30.280
I think we have to be very cognisant of the fact AI remains incredibly brittle

00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:35.800
It struggles to adapt to unexpected situations and scenarios...

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:39.560
...and warfare is just full of the unexpected

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:42.320
It’s replete with fog and friction

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:48.960
And in wars of the future, like now, not all fighting will be done with bullets and missiles

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.640
Over 8,000km from Ukraine, in the South China Sea...

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:00.680
...there may be fewer trenches, but a furious struggle is still under way

00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:05.000
China has long claimed sovereignty over Taiwan...

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:09.320
......a democratic, self-governing island that it sees as a breakaway province

00:13:09.640 --> 00:13:12.320
But geography is on Taiwan’s side

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:16.840
For China to launch a straightforward invasion of Taiwan, there are many difficulties...

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:22.320
...including launching the largest-ever amphibious operation since Normandy

00:13:22.760 --> 00:13:24.800
This will be extremely difficult for China

00:13:25.320 --> 00:13:28.280
The landing itself is also extremely difficult...

00:13:28.280 --> 00:13:30.560
...because China hasn’t actually conducted...

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:35.360
...any kinetic military operations since 1979, so this is a big gamble

00:13:38.120 --> 00:13:40.280
In the face of such challenges...

00:13:40.280 --> 00:13:44.080
...China may try a different tack if it invades Taiwan

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:49.760
The People’s Liberation Army believes in the three-warfare doctrine...

00:13:49.760 --> 00:13:53.720
...which requires communication with your potential audience...

00:13:53.720 --> 00:13:56.360
...in order to achieve a psychological effect

00:13:57.360 --> 00:14:02.840
Cyber provides a very powerful means for them to extend that concept...

00:14:02.840 --> 00:14:06.920
...such as propaganda, such as united front operations

00:14:06.920 --> 00:14:10.080
If they could do that, and this is relatively cheap...

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:13.320
...you could conduct an entire year’s worth of operations...

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:17.200
...and the cost would not nearly be as much as one ballistic missile

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:20.960
And cyberwarfare isn’t just a psychological weapon

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:25.080
They could cut off power supplies, critical military infrastructure...

00:14:25.080 --> 00:14:29.640
...such as surveillance radar stations and keep Taiwan blind and deaf...

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:32.040
...to facilitate further kinetic operations

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.760
It may sound like a cleaner version of future war...

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.120
...but when it comes to conflict, there’s no escape from attrition

00:14:40.600 --> 00:14:45.160
In Kyiv, Favorsky and his partner are preparing for him to return to the front

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:16.400
It’s very easy to get caught up in the hardware of this conflict...

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:19.760
...the drones, the tanks, the satellites

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:23.760
But it is very important to understand ultimately, this is still people

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.320
Mostly men, often very young men...

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:38.120
...holding guns, firing at the enemy and suffering trauma...

00:15:38.120 --> 00:15:40.000
...life-changing injuries and death

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:46.000
Even in a war with high levels of technological sophistication...

00:15:46.000 --> 00:15:48.400
...in the end, we still need to be prepared...

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:51.080
...for the 18-year-old dying somewhere in the mud

00:15:51.080 --> 00:15:57.000
The fundamental nature of war as a human conflict, with humans killing each other...

00:15:57.000 --> 00:15:59.840
...I don’t think that that is going to go away

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:03.320
War is still ultimately irreducibly...

00:16:03.320 --> 00:16:07.200
...about that clash of arms waged often at close quarters...

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.040
...in which bodies pile up...

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.560
...societies are ravaged

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:13.960
...and each country’s youth, the most talented of their young generation...

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:15.280
...are lost in huge numbers

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:20.600
Thank you for watching

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:23.640
To read more of our coverage on the future of warfare...

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.560
...please click on the link

00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:27.080
And don’t forget to subscribe

